# Rank 1403 / CVE-2022-31129: moment's RFC 2822 date parser stripped comments out
# of the input string with an unanchored global substitution whose negated
# character class admitted the comment's own opening delimiter, so the cost of
# `moment(str)` is quadratic in the length of the string (CWE-1333 inefficient
# regular expression complexity, CWE-400).
#
# `configFromRFC2822` (src/lib/create/from-string.js:189) calls
# `preprocessRFC2822(config._i)` on the raw string the caller passed to
# `moment()`, and that helper's first substitution is
# `s.replace(/\([^)]*\)|[\n\t]/g, ' ')`. `[^)]` excludes the closing parenthesis
# but not the opening one, so at every `(` in the subject the class runs to the
# end of the string and then gives a character back at a time looking for a `)`
# that is not there. A subject of n unclosed `(` costs O(n^2): each of the n
# start positions repeats the whole failing scan.
#
# The fixed revision is one character: `/\([^()]*\)|[\n\t]/g`. With the opening delimiter in
# the excluded set the class stops at the next `(`, so each opener is examined at
# most twice and the substitution is linear. Nothing else changes -- no length
# cap on the input, no replacement of the regex by a hand-written scan -- so the
# whole discriminator is the negated class, and it is a structural property of
# the pattern rather than of the operand.
#
# The pool row carries no flag and reads correctly:
# 9a3b5894f3d5d602948ac8a02e4ee528a49ca3a3 is the targeted one-line fix, the
# vulnerable class is present at its parent, and the remediation is present at
# the commit itself.
#
# What the detection keys on is the shape, not this pattern: a `String.prototype`
# regex operation whose pattern scans between a matched pair of ASCII bracket
# delimiters with a quantified negated class that excludes the closing delimiter
# and admits the opening one. Admitting the opener is what lets a failing scan run
# to the end of the subject and be repeated at every nested opener, so the family
# covers `<[^>]*>`, `\{[^}]*\}` and `\[[^\]]*\]` alongside this CVE's
# `\([^)]*\)`, in greedy and lazy spellings alike; each is quadratic on a run of
# unclosed openers and each is linear once the opener joins the excluded set.
# The label is the existing `code.RegexMatchSink` on the subject string, so the
# judgement stays where it is -- VYQL-DOS-002 requires a taint path from
# `core.UserControlledData` -- and no concept, rule or rule ID is added.
# `code.CatastrophicRegex` is considered and rejected for this: it is an
# ungated presence issue, and the ungated form of exactly this criterion is what
# rank 1343 measured at one real finding in seven to eleven. Gating on taint is
# the difference. Measured: the label lands 12 times across misp's
# app/webroot/js, microweber's bundled jquery-1.9.1.js, mattermost's
# recap_text_formatter.tsx, matrix-appservice-irc's src/irc/formatting.ts,
# marked/src and locutus/src, and adds zero findings on any of them -- the
# DOS-002 count is unchanged on misp (671 findings), microweber (71), mattermost
# (249), matrix-appservice-irc (97), locutus (215), mermaid (128), markdown-it
# (14), marked (6) and mediawiki-skins-Citizen (2). On moment's own src the count
# is a stable 2 -> 1 over three `--cache off` runs of each revision, and the
# finding that disappears is exactly the one on lib/create/from-string.js:153.
# The residual is src/test/helpers/common-locale.js:91, a real instance of the
# same shape (`/(\[[^\]]*\])/g`) in a test helper whose subject is moment's own
# locale data and is reached only through a `code.DomInput` source misattributed
# to that data.

test "rank1403: the RFC 2822 comment stripper admits the opening delimiter"
  lang javascript
  file src/lib/create/from-string.js
  profile library
  expect VYQL-DOS-002
  code
  ```
  export function preprocessRFC2822(s) {
      // Remove comments and folding whitespace and replace multiple-spaces with a single space
      return s
          .replace(/\([^)]*\)|[\n\t]/g, ' ')
          .replace(/(\s\s+)/g, ' ')
          .replace(/^\s\s*/, '')
          .replace(/\s\s*$/, '');
  }

  export function configFromRFC2822(config) {
      var match = rfc2822.exec(preprocessRFC2822(config._i));
      if (!match) {
          config._isValid = false;
      }
      return match;
  }
  ```

test "rank1403: excluding the opening delimiter makes the comment stripper linear"
  lang javascript
  file src/lib/create/from-string.js
  profile library
  reject VYQL-DOS-002
  code
  ```
  export function preprocessRFC2822(s) {
      // Remove comments and folding whitespace and replace multiple-spaces with a single space
      return s
          .replace(/\([^()]*\)|[\n\t]/g, ' ')
          .replace(/(\s\s+)/g, ' ')
          .replace(/^\s\s*/, '')
          .replace(/\s\s*$/, '');
  }

  export function configFromRFC2822(config) {
      var match = rfc2822.exec(preprocessRFC2822(config._i));
      if (!match) {
          config._isValid = false;
      }
      return match;
  }
  ```

# The same shape with a different delimiter pair and no moment API, to show the
# criterion is the bracket scan and not this CVE's pattern text.

test "rank1403: a tag stripper whose negated class admits the opening angle bracket"
  lang javascript
  file src/markup.js
  profile library
  expect VYQL-DOS-002
  code
  ```
  export function stripTags(markup) {
      return markup.replace(/<[^>]+>/gm, '');
  }
  ```

test "rank1403: the tag stripper excluding both angle brackets is clean"
  lang javascript
  file src/markup.js
  profile library
  reject VYQL-DOS-002
  code
  ```
  export function stripTags(markup) {
      return markup.replace(/<[^<>]+>/gm, '');
  }
  ```
